+A buffer larger than 4096 bytes is a known limit of this scheme: it still takes a 4096-byte
+granule, so only its first 4096 bytes of address space are its own. Its base is unique among live
+buffers and stable, like every base, but the synthetic address of an element more than 4096 bytes
+into it lies past its granule, where it can coincide with an address in another live buffer's
+range or in a granule handed out later. Only comparisons between such addresses are affected —
+an equality, an ordering or a difference of the integers, and so the ordering of two raw pointers
+into different buffers, which compares their addresses — never `ptr::eq`, which compares buffer
+and offset, nor a dereference. The limit is not fixed yet.
+
